Installed `SplitLines` (`@/components/ui/split-lines`) and the splitter it uses (`splitText` in `@/components/ui/split-lines-core`). No dependencies beyond React.

```tsx
import { SplitLines } from "@/components/ui/split-lines";

<SplitLines
  as="h2"
  options={{ type: ["lines"], mask: { lines: "0.25em" } }}
  onSplit={({ lines }, { resplit }) => {
    if (resplit) return;
    lines.forEach((line, i) =>
      line.animate(
        [{ transform: "translateY(100%)" }, { transform: "none" }],
        { duration: 900, delay: i * 100, easing: "cubic-bezier(0.23, 1, 0.32, 1)", fill: "backwards" },
      ),
    );
  }}
>
  Text that wraps
</SplitLines>
```

- Lines are the ones the browser painted (read with `Range.getClientRects()`), so hyphenation, `overflow-wrap`, `text-wrap: balance`, right-to-left text and Japanese or Thai come out as set. Words and characters sit inside those lines and keep their painted widths.
- It splits and stops: animate the units in `onSplit`, and skip the motion when the user prefers reduced motion. Each unit also carries `data-line` / `data-word` / `data-char` and a `--line` / `--word` / `--char` index for CSS staggers.
- A mask clips with `clip-path: inset(0)`; give it a reach (`{ lines: "0.25em" }`) for descenders, or clear the clip after the reveal.
- Without React, call `splitText(element, { type: ["lines", "words"] })` from `@/components/ui/split-lines-core`; it returns `{ lines, words, chars, masks, revert }`.
- For Motion variants and kerning-compensated character staggers, see Split Text.

